WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.332
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.188 --> 00:00:09.040
ENO COMPTON: I want to
start by making sure

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.190
that we're all on the same
page, that we understand what

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.410
"open cloud" means as a term.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.030
And I think it's totally
fair to ask, why do we care?

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:21.880
Why are we taking time
to talk about open cloud?

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.130
Before I do that,
though, this presentation

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.090
is really two parts.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:28.930
The first part is I want to walk
you through some of the things

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:30.910
the Go team has been
thinking about in regards

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.850
to open cloud and the
programming language Go.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.010
And then I want to
introduce to you

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.410
a new library from the Go team.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.970
So first, these questions here.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.100
What is open cloud,
and why do we care?

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.900
So let's talk about
open cloud first.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.420
The idea here is you
write your Go application

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.760
or your application, whatever.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.520
You pick it up and you
deploy it on one cloud.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.965
And the thinking is,
if you've deployed it

00:00:56.965 --> 00:01:00.640
or if you've written in a way
that follows kind of open cloud

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.130
principles, there should
be nothing that stops you

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.950
from picking up that
application and moving it over

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.370
and, on another cloud,
making minimal changes.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.820
And that's really the core
of what "open cloud" means

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.070
as I use the term today.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:20.140
"Portable" is another term that
you're perhaps familiar with.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:21.860
And why do we care?

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.520
Well, it's actually a really
nice thing, as developers,

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.130
to have the choice.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.639
When we have more
choices, we can

00:01:28.639 --> 00:01:30.430
do the thing that's
right for our business,

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.221
for our team, for our
application, whatever

00:01:32.221 --> 00:01:35.060
the case might be.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.550
And that's nice
because sometimes

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.530
Cloud A is what you
want, sometimes Cloud B

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:42.460
is what you want.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.940
Now, the diagram up here on
the slide has two clouds.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.872
You can imagine many clouds in a
kind of multi-cloud deployment.

00:01:49.872 --> 00:01:52.330
Some of you are perhaps running
some of your infrastructure

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:53.560
on premise.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.440
And this idea of an
open cloud application

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.050
is just as relevant
to a team that

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.510
wants to deploy some of their
infrastructure on premise

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.984
and some of their infrastructure
on the open cloud.

00:02:03.984 --> 00:02:05.400
For most of this
presentation, I'm

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.030
going to be talking about
it as if we're doing it

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.889
on public clouds,
but really, there's

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.530
no reason why you couldn't
do something similar

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.570
on an on-premise
deployment as well.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.570
So now that we've
kind of established

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.360
what the term "open cloud"
means for this presentation,

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.350
and why we care about it, and
why it's a good thing for us,

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.900
I want to stop and talk
about Go, the language,

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.700
and I want to talk
about the cloud.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:33.520
So Go is experiencing this
fantastic growth right now.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.650
There are lots and lots
of developers worldwide

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.025
who are choosing
the language for

00:02:37.025 --> 00:02:39.557
their critical infrastructure.

00:02:39.557 --> 00:02:41.140
And they're deploying
it on the cloud.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.120
There's a lot of fantastic
open source projects

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.140
that are written in Go.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:49.330
Kubernetes, Docker, Istio are a
couple of well-known examples.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.400
And it turns out, Go is
just such a fun language.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.983
I mean, I think, as
developers, that's

00:02:52.983 --> 00:02:56.356
probably the most important
thing-- it's fun to write Go.

00:02:56.356 --> 00:02:57.730
So as a result
right now, there's

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:01.904
an estimated 1 million
developers worldwide using Go.

00:03:01.904 --> 00:03:03.820
And many businesses are
choosing this language

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.500
to deploy their critical
infrastructure on it.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.050
And so it's just
a fantastic choice

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.820
for your cloud workloads.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.090
But recently the Go team has
been doing customer interviews

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.280
and thinking about
how we can make

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.680
Go a better language, how we
can make it a better ecosystem.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.520
What can we do to continue to
help drive the growth of Go?

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.759
And so when this concept
of open cloud came up,

00:03:26.759 --> 00:03:28.300
there was kind of
this open question.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.599
Like, what's Go's
relationship to open cloud?

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:32.140
I mean, we know that
it's a fantastic

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.854
language for the cloud,
but what about open cloud?

00:03:34.854 --> 00:03:37.270
What about this idea of being
able to write an application

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:38.728
that you can deploy
it on one cloud

00:03:38.728 --> 00:03:40.900
and pick it up and
deploy it on another

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:44.410
without any kind of significant
rewrites or anything like that?

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:48.890
And so that's the big guiding
question for this presentation.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:53.910
So why is developing open cloud
applications so difficult?

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.610
It turns out that right
now, of the applications

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:00.180
that are out in the world,
very few are the kind

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.430
that you can just move
from one cloud to another

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.290
without any kind
of major rewrite,

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.760
major kind of migration,
or anything like that.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.120
And so the question is,
why is this so difficult?

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.785
Why can't we just
do this right away?

00:04:11.785 --> 00:04:13.410
And if you think
about it, the answer's

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.780
actually fairly straightforward.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.750
From a high level, all clouds
are kind of similar, right?

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.430
We're talking about
virtual machines,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.410
we're talking about databases,
caches, queues, whatever.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.340
Like, it's all the kind of same
thing at the end of the day.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.090
But the thing is, when
you get down to it,

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.860
every cloud is actually pretty
different from the other cloud.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.900
And let's think
about why that is.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.302
Well, first of all, every
cloud offers some set

00:04:37.302 --> 00:04:38.760
of services that
aren't necessarily

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.250
the same with another cloud.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.730
Each of those services
has its own APIs.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:48.135
Maybe you're doing a REST API,
maybe you're doing a gRPC API.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.000
Next, each cloud
has its own SDK.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.240
So you have to learn that SDK.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.910
And SDKs are built to
be able to do everything

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.010
that's possible on a cloud.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:05.075
They're not necessarily tailored
to the application developer.

00:05:05.075 --> 00:05:06.950
So these are the things
that you want to use.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:10.060
It's kind of like
the kitchen sink.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.522
And so as a team, you spend
a bunch of time building

00:05:12.522 --> 00:05:13.980
ab understanding
of, like, what are

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:18.210
the APIs, what are the services,
what are the nuances of each

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:21.930
of those services, what are the
really useful parts of the SDK

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.060
that we want to use, and so on?

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.860
And you have to build
up that proficiency.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.440
It takes a while to
really have a strong grasp

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.840
of what the cloud can
do for your application,

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.600
or how to use the cloud
just right, and so on, such

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.030
that if you wanted
to pick up and put

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.804
your application
on another cloud,

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.470
you have this tremendous
learning curve.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.120
All that proficiency,
all that time

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.570
that you've invested as a team
to understand one cloud's APIs,

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:48.210
and SDKs, and so on
doesn't just immediately

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:51.209
transfer to another cloud.

00:05:51.209 --> 00:05:52.500
There's a learning curve there.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.020
And so what that
means is, as a team,

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.975
you get started on one cloud,
you're deploying your apps,

00:05:57.975 --> 00:05:59.850
you're building an
understanding of the APIs,

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:03.060
the services, the
nuances, and so on.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.880
And that results in that
you're probably still running

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:07.540
your application on that cloud.

00:06:10.331 --> 00:06:12.580
Chances are wherever you
started is where you're still

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.010
running your code today.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:18.460
So I imagine some of you in
the audience, or many of you,

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:21.580
are familiar with Kubernetes,
this really exciting

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.830
open source project
that's provided

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:28.710
a number of shared primitives
for container management.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.910
And so some of you
might be thinking, well,

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:33.804
doesn't Kubernetes already
make this possible?

00:06:33.804 --> 00:06:35.470
I mean, as long as
you have a Kubernetes

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:37.271
deployment and that
Kubernetes cluster,

00:06:37.271 --> 00:06:39.520
it doesn't really matter
where that cluster's running.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:41.777
So if you're on one
Kubernetes deployment,

00:06:41.777 --> 00:06:43.360
can't you just pick
up your deployment

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.570
and move it onto another
Kubernetes cluster?

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:49.420
And the answer,
actually, there is

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:51.610
using Kubernetes is a
fantastic first step

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:54.700
to having an open
cloud deployment,

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.530
but it's also not the last.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:59.980
And it's important to
think about why that is.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:04.110
So in kind of an
idealized world,

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.640
we might say an application
looks like this.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.950
You've written your
Go application,

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:10.720
you've put it
inside a container,

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.700
you've written some
YAML files such that you

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:14.332
can deploy it onto Kubernetes.

00:07:14.332 --> 00:07:15.790
And then that
Kubernetes cluster is

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.925
running on some compute resource
for some cloud provider.

00:07:18.925 --> 00:07:20.800
And so if you're thinking
about it like this,

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.508
you're thinking, well,
what's stopping me

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:24.430
from picking up my
Kubernetes deployment

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:26.980
and just putting it
on another cluster?

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:29.590
Isn't that open
cloud right there?

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:32.140
Well, as part of our customer
interviews-- and we've

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:33.880
been talking to a
lot and lots of teams

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:36.213
who are using Go, trying to
understand how they're using

00:07:36.213 --> 00:07:39.285
it, what kind of problems
they're running into,

00:07:39.285 --> 00:07:41.410
and just kind of the details
of how they're working

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:42.785
with the language--
we've learned

00:07:42.785 --> 00:07:45.040
that teams are under
an intense pressure

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:46.540
to be shipping features.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:48.040
At the end of the
day, that's really

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.650
what's valuable that developers
provide for the business--

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:53.560
we're able to ship great
features to our users.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.590
And so while these
teams are fully aware

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.530
of the importance of
separation of concerns

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:01.120
and loose coupling
between a cloud

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.359
provider and their
application, in practice,

00:08:03.359 --> 00:08:05.650
you have these really fantastic
services that the cloud

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:07.180
provider provides.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:11.170
We're talking databases, runtime
configuration, Blob storage,

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:13.247
these kind of things.

00:08:13.247 --> 00:08:14.830
They just end up
using those services,

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:16.390
because after all, they're
fantastic services,

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:17.800
you want to be shipping code.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:20.720
And so the application ends
up looking more like this.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.539
So rather than having this nice,
loose coupling between layers,

00:08:23.539 --> 00:08:25.330
you have your application
and it's actually

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:28.210
got these little hooks down
into the cloud provider.

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:29.830
So inside your
application code, you

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:31.750
have some particular
SDK invocations

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:34.480
that are maybe calling out
to the database, or the Blob

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.710
storage, or whatever.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:38.559
And so as a result,
your nice Go application

00:08:38.559 --> 00:08:41.334
now has this knowledge about
the underlying cloud provider,

00:08:41.334 --> 00:08:43.750
which really prevents you from
picking it up and moving it

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:47.070
onto another cloud.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.710
And so what that means is if
you want to write your app such

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:52.080
that you can deploy
it on multiple clouds,

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.020
you have kind of two
unpalatable choices.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.330
You can either rewrite
it, which we all

00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:58.720
know that's a tremendous risk.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.390
It's also very expensive.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:02.790
Or you can not do it at
all, which means, well,

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.950
now you've kind of thrown
the whole idea of having

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:08.137
a multi-cloud deployment
out the window.

00:09:08.137 --> 00:09:09.720
And this is one of
the major takeaways

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.190
we've heard from
a lot of teams who

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:15.750
are using Go in production for
their critical applications.

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.520
So let's think about this.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:18.570
We're developers.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:19.570
We solve problems.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:21.910
What can we do about this?

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:24.380
Well, as a developer,
you might say, well,

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:29.370
wouldn't it be nice if all cloud
providers had a fixed API such

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.645
that we write our code
to that particular API

00:09:32.645 --> 00:09:34.770
and then when we want to
put them in another cloud,

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:35.640
we can just go over there.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:36.931
We have a standard set of APIs.

00:09:36.931 --> 00:09:39.330
That would be kind of
nice, and it's probably not

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:40.110
going to happen.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.600
And that's actually pretty
fair, if you think about it.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.930
Every cloud provider is busy
building the best services they

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:48.210
can, the best SDKs they can.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:49.740
And they just don't
have time to be

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.240
looking over their shoulders,
thinking about standardization

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:53.040
efforts.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.644
So that idea is out the window.

00:09:56.644 --> 00:09:58.560
As a kind of side note,
it's also really great

00:09:58.560 --> 00:09:59.370
to have the choice.

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.260
I mean, again, the
whole idea of open cloud

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:02.697
is having the choice.

00:10:02.697 --> 00:10:05.280
You want to have the choice to
put your code on whatever cloud

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:07.404
makes sense to you, to your
team, to your business,

00:10:07.404 --> 00:10:08.440
and so on.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:10.440
And so it's super important
to have this ability

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:12.523
to freely choose, and it's
good for the ecosystem.

00:10:12.523 --> 00:10:14.850
And then we have these
diversity-of-service offerings,

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.260
and that makes life interesting.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:19.420
New service ideas are
popping up and so on.

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.240
So the next thought
we might have about,

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.960
how can we get this multi-cloud
deployment thing working,

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:27.480
is, well, why don't we identify
some generic interfaces?

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:29.710
Again, we know about
separation of concerns.

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:32.085
We know about loose coupling,
we know about encapsulation

00:10:32.085 --> 00:10:33.070
and all these things.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:36.330
So what if we looked at a
number of APIs across the clouds

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.070
that we're interested
in and tried

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.830
to identify some
shared APIs that

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:44.000
will work across all of them?

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.002
Well, it turns out--

00:10:45.002 --> 00:10:46.460
and this is, again,
something we've

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:48.335
learned from all of our
customer interviews--

00:10:48.335 --> 00:10:51.890
that's actually a
significant time investment.

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:53.420
And that means
you're spending time

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:56.210
trying to identify
design concerns

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:57.770
rather than just
shipping features

00:10:57.770 --> 00:10:59.330
to your users, which
is really what's

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:02.720
valuable at the end of the day.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:06.290
And again, because you have all
these disparate clouds with all

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:09.050
these disparate APIs and all
these nuances that are totally

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:11.600
different-- one cloud has
this particular nuance,

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:14.840
another cloud has that
particular nuance--

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:16.970
it's a non-trivial
task to identify

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:18.340
what those generic APIs are.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:22.680
I mean, it's doable, but again,
it's this big time investment.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.400
So again, you have
this pragmatic concern.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:28.170
You just don't have
time to do that.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:30.530
So that's where the
Go Cloud team thought,

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:32.930
we need to have something
better to fix this problem.

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:35.630
We need to have a library that
makes it easy for developers

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:39.147
who are choosing Go to write
open cloud applications such

00:11:39.147 --> 00:11:41.480
that they don't have to throw
portability out the window

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:42.730
when they choose the language.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:44.630
They have this ecosystem
that supports them.

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:50.450
So we just open-sourced Go Cloud
yesterday afternoon, I think.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:54.000
And in the core, it's a library
and tools for open cloud

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:54.810
development in Go.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.390
So what does that mean exactly?

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.320
Well, first of all, we have
this state of the world

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:02.510
right now, right,
where your application

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:05.450
is coded to your
particular cloud provider.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:07.820
The idea of Go Cloud
here is that same idea

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.820
of separation of concerns,
where you write your Go

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:15.440
application, calling into these
portable APIs for Go Cloud.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:19.130
And then Go Cloud has these
cloud-specific definitions

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:21.800
for your database, for
your runtime configuration,

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:24.260
for your Blob storage,
such that when

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:26.600
you want to switch
out clouds or you

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.700
want to deploy on
multiple clouds,

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:32.630
you have this very clear seam in
between your application logic

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:34.580
and your setup code
that's actually

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:38.810
working with all these
particular, specific clouds.

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:41.870
So we're starting with some
generic APIs for some commonly

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:43.470
used services.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:47.270
That includes Blob storage,
MySQL database access,

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:49.940
runtime configuration,
and we're also

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:53.030
releasing an HTTP server that's
configured with distributed

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:55.820
tracing, health checks,
and request logging,

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:59.571
something that we think every
Go developer is going to need.

00:12:59.571 --> 00:13:01.820
The project's open source
on GitHub, like I mentioned.

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:03.486
It's released under
an Apache 2 license.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:08.630
As an engineer, though, I'd
love to tell you more about it,

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:09.740
but honestly, I
think the best way

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:11.340
to communicate what
Go Cloud really

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.220
is is to show you some code.

