00:15:23.227 --> 00:15:24.810
And then we have
some setup code here.

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:28.690
And this calls to a setup
bucket function, which takes

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:31.000
in the cloud, the bucket name.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.320
We'll get to that in a moment.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:35.560
But then once we have
the bucket set up,

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:38.740
we're then using Go's
generic I/O utilities

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:43.150
to read in a file from
the local file system,

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:47.140
create a writer onto
this new bucket,

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:51.580
and then writing to it using
Go's standard io.writer

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:54.610
interface, and then
closing it out.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:56.440
And if you have
looked at Go before,

00:15:56.440 --> 00:16:00.860
this will look very much like
opening up a file to you.

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:04.780
So a lot of the magic of
what Go Cloud is doing

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.680
is all in this Setup
Bucket function,

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:08.080
so let's take a look at that.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:11.940
And that's in our setup.go file.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:15.480
So you'll notice that this
imports our Go Cloud packages.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:20.200
It also imports the
AWS packages as well.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:23.190
So in our Setup Bucket
function, you'll

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:27.330
notice that it's returning
blob.bucket, this generic type

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:30.630
that is coming from Go Cloud.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:35.040
We are able to just switch
between each individual cloud

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.750
and return something
that implements

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.760
this generic blob.bucket type.

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:44.940
So in our Setup AWS
function, which we just ran,

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:49.170
it gets credentials, and does
other cloud-specific things,

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:53.100
and then calls the
S3 Blob open bucket

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:55.110
to get the particular
implementation that's

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:58.000
the generic one that we want.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:01.870
We can do the exact same
thing with GCS and GCP.

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:05.730
So in this case, we start
off with getting the GCP

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:07.200
credentials.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:10.950
We create an HTTP client
that's authenticated with this.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:15.720
And then we finally just
create or open a new bucket

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:18.970
that's configured for GCS.

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:22.710
So as Eno mentioned before,
the critical part of this

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:24.690
is that what Go
Cloud is giving you

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:29.580
is this seam between having your
cloud-specific code, your cloud

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:34.950
provider-specific code, and your
generic, abstract application

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:36.550
logic.

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:40.500
So let's go ahead
and see how we'd

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.580
